"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,416\nEpisode 11\n\n3\n00:00:07,496 --> 00:00:09,565\nSpacing and sleep\n\n4\n00:00:12,365 --> 00:00:15,464\nMemory fades fast at first,\n\n5\n00:00:15,875 --> 00:00:17,001\nthen more slowly.\n\n6\n00:00:17,021 --> 00:00:19,740\nEbbinghaus mapped this curve in the 1880s.\n\n7\n00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:22,280\nEach review lifts the curve back up,\n\n8\n00:00:22,376 --> 00:00:24,501\nand afterward it falls more slowly.\n\n9\n00:00:24,942 --> 00:00:26,444\nThat's the spacing effect.\n\n10\n00:00:26,834 --> 00:00:28,716\nThe same practice, spread out,\n\n11\n00:00:28,917 --> 00:00:31,484\nlasts longer than practice crammed together.\n\n12\n00:00:31,654 --> 00:00:34,877\nIt's one of the most replicated findings in psychology.\n\n13\n00:00:35,371 --> 00:00:38,488\nOne review found eight hundred thirty-nine tests of it,\n\n14\n00:00:38,606 --> 00:00:40,828\nin more than three hundred experiments.\n\n15\n00:00:41,249 --> 00:00:42,732\nIn a large follow-up,\n\n16\n00:00:42,938 --> 00:00:46,982\nthe best gap between sessions grew with how long you needed to remember.\n\n17\n00:00:47,393 --> 00:00:48,263\nFor a test a week away,\n\n18\n00:00:48,370 --> 00:00:50,531\none to three days.\n\n19\n00:00:50,870 --> 00:00:53,415\nFor a year, about three to five weeks.\n\n20\n00:00:53,585 --> 00:00:55,249\nBut spacing feels worse.\n\n21\n00:00:55,619 --> 00:00:57,062\nIn a flashcard study,\n\n22\n00:00:57,222 --> 00:01:00,375\nspacing beat cramming for ninety percent of learners.\n\n23\n00:01:00,709 --> 00:01:04,397\nYet seventy-two percent believed cramming had worked better.\n\n24\n00:01:04,567 --> 00:01:06,215\nSleep is part of the schedule.\n\n25\n00:01:06,645 --> 00:01:12,212\nIn rats, place cells that fired together while exploring fire together again during sleep,\n\n26\n00:01:12,556 --> 00:01:14,133\nas if replaying the day.\n\n27\n00:01:14,747 --> 00:01:19,210\nIn people, sleeping between two study sessions meant faster relearning,\n\n28\n00:01:19,456 --> 00:01:21,618\nand better recall six months later.\n\n29\n00:01:22,065 --> 00:01:26,466\nAnd one night without sleep cut the hippocampus's response during new learning.\n\n30\n00:01:26,636 --> 00:01:27,752\nTwo cautions.\n\n31\n00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:31,349\nThe evidence is strongest for facts and vocabulary.\n\n32\n00:01:31,681 --> 00:01:34,503\nFor complex skills, results are more mixed.\n\n33\n00:01:34,726 --> 00:01:36,721\nAnd many sleep studies are small.\n\n34\n00:01:37,057 --> 00:01:39,249\nTreat them as consistent signals,\n\n35\n00:01:39,269 --> 00:01:40,759\nnot precise numbers.\n\n36\n00:01:40,929 --> 00:01:41,716\nFor teaching.\n\n37\n00:01:42,113 --> 00:01:44,519\nBring old material back in later weeks,\n\n38\n00:01:44,693 --> 00:01:46,702\nnot only in the unit where it was taught.\n\n39\n00:01:47,125 --> 00:01:50,221\nMake exams cumulative, so spacing pays off.\n\n40\n00:01:50,567 --> 00:01:52,936\nStart practice early and return to it.\n\n41\n00:01:53,310 --> 00:01:56,203\nSet deadlines that don't reward all-nighters.\n\n42\n00:01:56,508 --> 00:01:59,791\nAnd tell students plainly that sleep is part of studying.\n\n43\n00:01:59,961 --> 00:02:02,267\nLearning doesn't stop when the session ends.\n\n44\n00:02:02,582 --> 00:02:04,040\nTime apart, and sleep,\n\n45\n00:02:04,151 --> 00:02:05,600\nare where it settles in.\n\n"
